  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(154.82pt, 98.06pt, " remains largely unexplored. The quest to quantify this information dates back ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 112.7pt, "to Spänkuch [3], who qualitatively identified “sensitive regions” in extinction measurements ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 127.34pt, "where the information content regarding particle size is maximized. However, a rigorous ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 141.98pt, "quantitative explanation for why these regions exist remained elusive. This gap in understanding ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 156.62pt, "prompts a critical question for modern spectroscopy: What are the fundamental limits of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 171.26pt, "information density within a ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(220.85pt, 171.26pt, "𝑄")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(228.65pt, 173.66pt, "𝑒𝑥𝑡")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(242.69pt, 171.26pt, " spectrum, and how does this complexity scale across the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 185.9pt, "diverse dielectric media? ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 208.58pt, "We demonstrate that the answer to this scaling lies in a universal information bottleneck that ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 223.22pt, "occurs at the onset of the Mie transition (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(288.29pt, 223.22pt, "𝑟≈0.1 𝜇𝑚")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(345.07pt, 223.22pt, "). At this peak of spectral entropy, the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 237.86pt, "transition from Rayleigh power-law scaling to complex interference patterns reaches its maximum ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 252.5pt, "structural complexity [4]. This study defines the information bottleneck as the critical coordinate ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 267.29pt, "where the sparsity assumptions required for compressed sensing are most severely tested [5, 6, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 281.93pt, "7]. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 304.49pt, "This bottleneck represents the maximum number of effective degrees of freedom required to ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 319.13pt, "represent a material's optical response [8, 9]. While traditional estimates of the number of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 333.77pt, "degrees of freedom often overlook the impact of material contrast, recent analysis has shown ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 348.53pt, "that the degree of freedom is intrinsically linked to the scatterer’s dielectric properties in ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 363.17pt, "nonlinear inverse problems [10]. Interestingly, our previous work demonstrated that model ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 377.81pt, "biological cells and ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(171.14pt, 377.81pt, "7")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(177.74pt, 377.81pt, "-component polymer mixtures could be deconvolved from a single ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(518.26pt, 377.81pt, "𝑄")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(526.06pt, 380.21pt, "𝑒𝑥𝑡")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 392.45pt, "spectrum with near-perfect accuracy [11, 12, 13, 14]. While such high-fidelity reconstruction of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 407.09pt, "high-dimensional mixtures appeared to surpass expected information-theoretic limits, by ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 421.73pt, "accounting for this contrast-dependent dimensionality, we reveal that the ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(456.67pt, 421.73pt, "𝑄")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(464.5pt, 424.13pt, "𝑒𝑥𝑡")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(478.54pt, 421.73pt, " manifold is ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 436.39pt, "characterized by an inherent low-dimensionality. Consistent with the theory of structural ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 451.03pt, "invertibility [15], we show that the system’s “effective dimension” matches the available degree ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 465.67pt, "of freedom at the Mie transition, providing the mathematical justification for the success of such ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 480.31pt, "counter-intuitive inverse reconstructions.  ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 502.99pt, "In this regime, the choice of a mathematical basis is not merely an optimization but a prerequisite ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 517.63pt, "for physical integrity [16]. A non-compressible representation results in an irrecoverable loss of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 532.27pt, "information, exacerbating the ill-posed nature of inverse problems [13, 14, 11, 12] and leading to ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 546.91pt, "numerical instabilities that confound modeling [17, 18, 19]. Unlike data-driven models, such as ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 561.55pt, "Principal Component Analysis (PCA) [20] or Proper Orthogonal Decomposition (POD) [21], which ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 576.19pt, "require extensive training and lack a closed-form physical interpretation, or Wavelets [22] that ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 590.83pt, "excel at local discontinuities, ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(222.77pt, 590.83pt, "𝑄")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(230.57pt, 593.23pt, "𝑒𝑥𝑡")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(244.61pt, 590.83pt, " profiles require a basis that can resolve global harmonic ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 605.5pt, "oscillations without introducing artifacts. We show that the conventional fast Fourier transform ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 620.14pt, "(FFT) [23] is physically mismatched for this task; its periodic boundary assumptions induce ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 634.9pt, "spectral leakage [24] that forces a massive expansion in the basis set to resolve fine-scale Mie ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 649.54pt, "ripples. Standard windowing techniques [25, 26] or parametric methods [27] can mitigate this ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 664.18pt, "leakage for frequency identification, but they remain suboptimal for signal reconstruction, as they ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 678.82pt, "either suppress the physically meaningful oscillations at the boundaries or require ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(502.66pt, 678.82pt, "a priori")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 693.456pt, "assumptions about the underlying model. While experimentalists have successfully used the FFT-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 708.096pt, "based compressed sensing to accelerate nano-FTIR imaging [5], we find that these sub-sampling ")
