  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 15.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#0f4761"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 433.39pt, "Spectral Signal Conditioning and Orthogonal Transformation ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 453.31pt, "To ensure a standardized analysis of the extinction manifold, the simulated ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(450.43pt, 453.31pt, "𝑄")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(458.23pt, 455.71pt, "𝑒𝑥𝑡")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(472.3pt, 453.31pt, " spectra were ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 467.95pt, "interpolated onto a uniform wavelength grid of ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(299.33pt, 467.95pt, "𝑁= 620")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(343.99pt, 467.95pt, " points. Before transformation, the signal ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 482.59pt, "mean was removed to isolate the oscillatory features of the Mie resonances from the DC offset, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 497.23pt, "focusing the analysis on the structural complexity of the scattering profile. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 519.91pt, "We evaluated the compressibility of the extinction manifold using two distinct orthogonal bases: ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 534.55pt, "the discrete cosine transform (DCT) and the fast Fourier transform (FFT). This comparison is ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 549.19pt, "central to our hypothesis that the periodic assumptions of Fourier analysis are physically ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 563.83pt, "inconsistent with non-periodic optical spectra. To test if this inconsistency could be mitigated ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 578.47pt, "through standard signal processing techniques, the FFT was evaluated both in its raw form and in ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 593.11pt, "conjunction with a Hann window [41]. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 615.82pt, "Critically, we hypothesized that windowing—while effective at suppressing spectral leakage in ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 630.46pt, "frequency identification—is inherently destructive for ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(332.23pt, 630.46pt, "𝑄")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(340.03pt, 632.86pt, "𝑒𝑥𝑡")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(354.07pt, 630.46pt, " reconstruction. Because the boundary ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 645.1pt, "values of an extinction spectrum encode essential physical information regarding the scattering ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 659.74pt, "limits, the tapering effect of a Hann window attenuates meaningful oscillations, potentially ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 674.38pt, "degrading the integrity of inverse modeling. In contrast, the DCT’s even-symmetric boundary ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 689.02pt, "conditions provide a naturally consistent basis that preserves boundary information without the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 703.656pt, "need for artificial windowing. ")
